(Bug #70: `append each <var> from <collection> treating <match> as
 <replacement> to <list>` parsed the clause and threw it away, so every
 element arrived unsubstituted. The clause now reaches the appended value,
 the way `print each ... treating ...` has always carried it.)

(From a list held in a variable - the register's repro)
a list called names is ["ann", "-"].
a list called out is [].
append each name from names treating "-" as "anon" to out.
print out.

(From a list literal, and over numbers as well as text)
a list called kept is [].
append each score from [1, 2] treating 1 as 9 to kept.
print kept.

(The control: no clause, so nothing is substituted)
a list called plain is [].
append each name from names to plain.
print plain.

(A `but if` branch takes the clause on the base action, exactly as a
 branch under `print each ... treating ...` does)
a list called branched is [].
append each name from names treating "-" as "anon" to branched, but if name is "ann" append "AA".
print branched.
